## Runtime: part of the AIL native runtime; linked into user executables
## under the Runtime Exception (see LICENSE).
##
## The primitives of the native runtime (native-abi.md §6, ADR 0387). The
## backend expands each call inline; these declarations only let the
## bootstrap check the runtime's source. Calling them anywhere but in the
## runtime is not possible: the backend knows them only there.

## The 8 bytes at address `a`.
fn load64(a int) int
    todo

fn store64(a int, v int)
    todo

## The byte at `a`, 0-255.
fn load8(a int) int
    todo

fn store8(a int, v int)
    todo

fn loadf(a int) float
    todo

fn storef(a int, x float)
    todo

## A raw system call (native-abi.md §4); the result register.
fn syscall(n int, a int, b int, c int, d int, e int, f int) int
    todo

## The address of the runtime's global slot `k` (8 bytes each).
fn global(k int) int
    todo

## The address of a string object.
fn str_addr(s str) int
    todo

## A string at an address.
fn str_at(p int) str
    todo

## A list's address, and a list at an address.
fn list_addr(xs List[int]) int
    todo

fn list_at(p int) List[int]
    todo

fn float_bits(x float) int
    todo

fn bits_float(n int) float
    todo

## `x` rounded to the nearest `f32` (ties to even) and widened back: the
## single-precision result of one operation done in `float` (a `float` holds
## the exact product, sum or quotient of two `f32` to twice the precision
## needed, so rounding once gives the correctly rounded `f32`).
fn f32_round(x float) float
    todo

## Store the callee-saved registers at `buf` (x86-64: 5 words, ARM64: 10) and
## return the stack pointer. The collector's view of the registers.
fn gc_regs(buf int) int
    todo

## The address after the last global slot: the runtime's and the program's.
fn globals_end() int
    todo

## The target: 0 Linux x86-64, 1 Linux ARM64, 2 macOS ARM64.
fn target() int
    todo

## The address of the call table (`trace.tab`, trace.frame.1).
fn code_tab() int
    todo

## The frame pointer: `[fp]` is the caller's, `[fp + 8]` the return address.
fn frame_ptr() int
    todo

## Atomic operations on the 8 bytes at address `a`, sequentially consistent
## (ADR 0600): load, store, exchange (the old value), add (the old value),
## and compare-and-swap (the value found: `old` means it swapped).
fn atomic_load(a int) int
    todo

fn atomic_store(a int, v int)
    todo

fn atomic_xchg(a int, v int) int
    todo

fn atomic_add(a int, n int) int
    todo

fn atomic_cas(a int, old int, new int) int
    todo

## The stack pointer.
fn sp() int
    todo

## A new thread (ADR 0599): runs `rt.worker_main(arg)` on the stack whose
## 16-byte aligned top is `top`, shares memory with its creator, and returns
## in the creator its thread id (negative on failure). Linux only.
fn clone(top int, arg int) int
    todo

## Continue this thread as `rt.worker_main(arg)` on a fresh stack at `top`,
## abandoning its frames. Never returns.
fn restart(top int, arg int)
    todo

## Return from a signal handler to the interrupted code: `rt_sigreturn` on
## Linux x86-64, where the function holding it was entered by a `ret` from the
## handler (ADR 1011). It restores the stack pointer to the signal frame, then
## makes the call. Never returns; x86-64 Linux only.
fn sigreturn()
    todo

## The first argument the caller passed in `rcx` (the Win64 convention), for a
## function the system calls back (ADR 1011). Read it first in the function,
## before anything else can use the register. Windows x86-64 only.
fn win_arg0() int
    todo

## Call the libSystem function `sym` (a string literal, with its leading
## underscore) with integer arguments through a pointer the loader binds;
## gives its result (native-abi.md §11). Declared with four arguments, but the
## lowering takes any count: call sites pass what the function needs (the
## generators place arguments as for any call, so as many as they support).
## Targets without the library trap, so guard it with `target() == 2`
## (or the Windows target).
fn import_call(sym str, a int, b int, c int, d int) int
    todo

## The address of the function named by the string literal `sym`.
fn fn_addr(sym str) int
    todo
